<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Farshut</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Farshut is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qena">Qena</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> bend at Nag Hammadi. In the 17th and 18th centuries it was the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawwara">Hawwara</a> Arab sheikhs, who ruled much of Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> as tax farmers under Ottoman Egypt and reached their height under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammam-ibn-yusuf">Shaykh Hammam</a> ibn Yusuf. Their power collapsed in 1769, when the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-bey-al-kabir">Ali Bey al-Kabir</a> defeated them.</p></div>
